\description{
Turn phrases, spaCy-style pattern lists or a data.frame of patterns into a
\code{spacyr_entity_patterns} object: a checked set of rules for spaCy's
\code{entity_ruler}, which finds named entities by matching phrases or sequences
of tokens. Creating the object needs neither Python nor spaCy; pass it to
\code{\link[=spacy_initialize]{spacy_initialize()}} as \code{entity_patterns} to add the rules to the pipeline.
}
\details{
Token patterns are checked for their structure only: that each is a list
with one named list per token. spaCy checks the attribute names, operators
and values when the patterns are added to a pipeline, and reports any
errors then.

Two conversions make patterns from R reach spaCy in the form it expects.
The values of spaCy's set operators (\code{IN}, \code{NOT_IN}, \code{IS_SUBSET},
\code{IS_SUPERSET} and \code{INTERSECTS}) are always stored as lists, so that
\code{list(LOWER = list(IN = "dr"))} stays a list of one value, rather than
becoming a single string. Whole numbers stored as doubles, such as \code{3}
in \code{list(LENGTH = 3)}, become integers, since spaCy does not accept
decimal numbers in token patterns.

Phrases, including dictionary values without wildcards, are split into
tokens by the pipeline's own tokenizer, and some of its rules depend on
case: spaCy's English models keep \code{"Inc."} as one token, but split
\code{"inc."} into \code{"inc"} and \code{"."}. So write phrases in their usual case, even with
\code{case_insensitive = TRUE}, and make dictionaries with
\code{quanteda::dictionary(tolower = FALSE)}, since by default it lowercases
their values.

Patterns match spaCy's tokens, not characters. For instance, spaCy's
English models make "Prof." a single token, so a pattern for academic
titles needs to list both \code{"prof"} and \code{"prof."}, to match titles written
with and without a full stop.
}
\examples{
